  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nCyclonic flow aloft will prevail today with a cold front pushing\noffshore this morning. A broken line of showers and perhaps a\nrumble will push southeast of the mountains through day break \nand then offshore between 7 am and 9 am. QPF with these showers \nwill be light and generally less than a tenth of an inch. Winds \nwill turn westerly behind the front and will increase late this \nmorning into the afternoon when gusts will approach 25-30 mph. \nDeep mixing to 800 mb along with some downsloping component will\nallow highs to climb into the upper 60s to low 70s south of the\nmountains. Subtle height falls combined with surface heating \nwill allow for MU CAPE to approach 500 J/kg with most CAMs \nsuggesting an isolated thunderstorm will be possible across the \ninterior this afternoon. Gusty winds will subside this evening \nas a surface inversion develops with the loss of heating. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nCyclonic flow aloft continues on Friday while the pressure\ngradient strengthens over the area between incoming high\npressure and low pressure deepening over Atlantic Canada. A\nsurface trough crossing the area will bring chances for showers\nand perhaps a thunderstorm, mainly across the north and during\nthe afternoon. The main story on Friday will be the gusty WNW\nfrom mid morning into the afternoon. BUFKIT profiles show \nmixing to 700 mb with winds at the top of the mixed layer around\n40 kts while mean layer momentum transfer is 25-30 kts. This \nshould lead to persistent surface gusts up to 30 mph with \npotential for occasional stronger gusts. Otherwise, skies will \nbe mostly sunny south of the mountains with highs ranging from \nnear 50F across the north to mid 60s along the coast and \nsouthern NH.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest forecast guidance continues to indicate that remnant moisture \nfrom Tropical Cyclone Isaias combined with an approaching front to \nour west will bring a chance for rainfall to the area late Sunday \ninto Columbus Day. There remains rather significant uncertainty in \nterms of rainfall timing along with rainfall amounts though, with \nsome guidance bringing meaningful rainfall across much of our area \nwhile other guidance keeps it mostly to our south. Latest NAEFS \nguidance brings precipitable water values (PWATs) up to around 1.50\" \nby Monday morning, which is ~+2 standard deviation anomaly. That \nbeing said, latest AI and ensemble probabilities for 24-hr QPF of \n>=0.50\" are generally 25% or less (highest across southern areas) \nand therefore any rain that we manage to get from this system looks \nvery manageable and beneficial.  \n\nAny lingering rain should end Monday evening with mainly dry \nconditions through the first half of Tuesday likely before another \ncold front and trof of low pressure crosses late day/night and \nbrings renewed shower chances. Falling temperatures aloft may allow \nsome of this to fall as snow across the higher terrain and towards \nthe Canadian Border as low temperatures fall into the 30s to near 40 \ndegrees. Colder temperatures look to follow behind this front for \nthe middle to end of next week.",
